// Drift check between the engine's types (Rust, the source of truth) and `types.ts` (RFC 027 §7).
//
// Every canonical example in `benchmark/schema/fixtures/` (generated by the engine's
// `schema_is_current` test) is imported here and checked two ways:
//
// - at compile time (`tsc -b`, run by `pnpm build`): each fixture `satisfies` its TS type, read
//   loosely (a JSON import widens string literals to `string`), and has no key the TS type lacks,
//   at any depth (`exactly`). A field Rust adds, drops, renames or makes nullable fails the build
//   and names the path;
// - at run time (vitest): literal unions (codes, states, phases, enums) hold allowed values, and
//   top-level keys match the TS type. Fixtures added later are matched by file-name prefix and get
//   the run-time checks; add them to the imports below to type-check them too.
//
// Workload `results` are checked too: each `report-<workload>-ok|failed.json` against
// `RunReportOf<workload>`, and each per-mode example `report-<mode>.json` against its exact results
// type (`TcpLatencyResults`, `VideoResults`, …), so every results field is typed, present and
// nothing more.

